# Vault — build-time configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored.
#
# These are OAuth client identifiers, kept out of the repository so that each
# person who builds the app points it at their own Google Cloud project. The
# client ID for either platform is not a secret. The desktop client's secret
# (see below) isn't meaningfully one either — PKCE is what actually secures
# the exchange — but Google's token endpoint requires it be present, so it has
# to be here too.
#
# With every value unset the app still builds and runs: it stays local-only
# and the Settings screen reports that Google Drive is not configured.
#
# See docs/google-oauth-setup.md for how to obtain these.

# OAuth client of type "Desktop app" in the `vault` Google Cloud project
# (name it whatever you like — this is just this file's own suggestion).
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_DESKTOP=

# The "Client secret" Google's Console shows for that same Desktop-app client.
# Google issues one even for this installed-app client type, and its token
# endpoint rejects a request that omits it ("client_secret is missing"), even
# though PKCE alone is what actually secures a public client like this one.
# Treat it exactly like the client ID above, not as something requiring its
# own protection.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_DESKTOP=

# OAuth client of type "Android" in the same project. Requires the package name
# `com.passhandler.app` plus the SHA-1 of the signing key. Debug and release
# keys have different fingerprints and need separate clients — set this to the
# debug client while developing and swap it for the release client when you cut
# a build to hand out. Google issues no client secret for an Android client, so
# there is no corresponding VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET_ANDROID.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID_ANDROID=
